/*
  ============================================================================
   VESSEL MEASUREMENT RECORDER (VMR)
   Ship motion, stability, waves, position, location marks / CTD casts and
   weather, logged to microSD with a GPS-disciplined clock. OLED screens, an
   optional 7-segment and 20x4 LCD, physical controls, alarms (an anchor
   watch, BNWAS and limits, on an optional piezo and vibration motor), and a
   Wi-Fi hotspot dashboard on any phone or laptop. With SeaLabMOS it also runs the
   WTB (wet-talk board) and IWB (in-water board): a CTD on a tether, and six
   Niskin bottles fired from the dashboard or the WTB.

   Aspect Wave VMR(TM). Copyright (c) 2026 Aspect Wave LLC. GPL-3.0-or-later: see LICENSE.
   Version history: CHANGELOG.md. What it cannot do: KNOWN_LIMITATIONS.md.
   Reading the CSV files: vmr_reader.py. The VMR / WTB / IWB link:
   VMR_WTB_IWB_Interface_Spec. Wiring: the VMR wiring sheet.
   Tabs: VMR.ino (this), slm.ino (SeaLabMOS), slm_types.h, vmr_link.h (kept
   identical to the WTB's copy).
  ============================================================================

  HARDWARE
    - Adafruit ESP32-S2 Feather with w.FL antenna (MAX17048 fuel gauge, 0x36)
    - Adalogger FeatherWing: PCF8523 RTC (0x68), microSD (CS pin 10)
    - Ultimate GPS FeatherWing (PA1616D) on Serial1, 9600 baud
    - FeatherWing OLED 128x64 (SH1107, 0x3C); button B = pin 6 (A and C not
      used: pin 9 takes the Mark LED or the relay, pin 5 the PCF8574 INT wire)
    - 24LC32 I2C EEPROM (0x50) and BNO085 9-DOF IMU (0x4A), STEMMA QT
    - Controls, on the I2C boards by default (the Pin mappings card can move them):
        PCF8574 (0x20): P0 pot switch, P1 screen button, P2 Mark button;
          P3-P7 free for the optional buttons (Cast, Record, Pause,
          Wi-Fi hotspot, Screen jump; each None until picked in the Pin mappings card)
          (each pin -> button / switch -> GND; switch closed = displays on)
        ADS7830 (0x48): channel 0 = the 10K linear pot's wiper (ends to
          VIN and GND)
      The screen button and pot switch can instead be on Feather pins 12
      and 11 (to GND). The pot is always on the ADS7830.
    - Latching power switch between the battery and the Feather
    - Indicator LEDs Mark, Recording, Fault (pins in the Pin mappings card):
        on the AW9523 LED driver (Adafruit 4886, 0x58-0x5B, the Add-ons card):
          constant-current sinks dimmed in 256 steps, about 18 mA at full
          (ISEL 18.5 mA): long leg -> the AW9523's VIN (3.3 V), short leg ->
          its pin, no resistor. The wiring sheet uses P0_0 / P0_1 / P0_2.
        or on Feather pins (firmware default Mark pin 9, Recording A3, Fault
          A4; any of A0-A5, pin 9 or 13): pin -> 1-2.2 kOhm -> LED -> GND,
          PWM dimmed. A5 only with the wind meter off; A4 and pin 9 never
          with SeaLabMOS (the relay), the laptop port's pins never.
        or on PCF8574 pins: on / off only.
    - Optional wind meter: Calypso ULP (RS485, NMEA 0183) through a SparkFun
      SP3485 breakout at 3.3 V: TX-O -> pin 12, RX-I -> pin 11, RTS -> A5;
      ULP green -> A, yellow -> B, brown -> 3V, white -> GND. Pins 11 and 12
      are then not controls, and A5 not an LED.
    - Optional displays: Adafruit 878 4-digit 7-segment (HT16K33, 0x70-0x77);
      Adafruit 498 RGB 20x4 LCD (all 4 rows) on the 292 I2C backpack
      (MCP23008, 0x20-0x27: solder A0 for 0x21 when the PCF8574 is at 0x20).
      The backlight is common anode with resistors on the LCD: pin 15 ->
      3.3 V, pins 16 / 17 / 18 (R / G / B) -> the pins picked in the Pin mappings card:
      AW9523 pins (the sheet uses P0_3 / P0_4 / P0_5) and Feather pins
      dim and mix colours, PCF8574 pins only switch them. Firmware default
      A0 / A1 / A2.
    - Optional I2C add-ons (the Add-ons card): BMP388 pressure (0x77 / 0x76; its own
      temperature is the box temperature), SHT30 or SHT40 air temperature
      and humidity (0x44 / 0x45, found by itself), AW9523 (above).
    - Optional alarm outputs (see ALARMS): a passive piezo (a bare disc or
      Adafruit 1739 / 1740) on one Feather pin to GND, or across two pins
      driven in opposite phase (the Pin mappings card); a DRV2605L haptic
      driver (Adafruit 2305, 0x5A, the Add-ons card) with a vibrating mini
      motor disc (1201).
    - SeaLabMOS (the Add-ons card; see slm.ino): Adafruit Latching Mini Relay
      FeatherWing (SET A4, UNSET pin 9) switching the 12 V to the WTB and
      IWB; INA260 (0x40) on the 12 V battery (volts, amps, % from the
      chemistry in the System card); optional laptop port, a Wiz5500 Ethernet breakout
      on SPI (CS default A0, INT optional), at http://169.254.1.1. The WTB is
      reached over ESP-NOW on the hotspot's channel.
    Feather A0-A5, 9 and 13 are outputs only. No analog input is read on the
    Feather: A0-A4 are on ADC2, unreliable while Wi-Fi runs.
    The PCF8574's INT pin -> Feather pin 5 lets its buttons wake the VMR from
    Datalogger sleep (open drain; pin 5's pull-up holds it high).
    I2C runs at 400 kHz.
    GPS antenna: "external" shows only for an active antenna (it draws
    current). The GPS wing's red LED blinks every 1 s while searching, every
    15 s with a fix.

  ARDUINO IDE SETUP
    Board: "Adafruit Feather ESP32-S2" (esp32 by Espressif, core 3.3.x)
    Partition Scheme: "Minimal SPIFFS (1.9MB APP with OTA/128KB SPIFFS)" - for
      firmware updates over Wi-Fi. "TinyUF2 No OTA" runs everything else but
      has no spare slot for an update (the dashboard says so).
    Libraries: Adafruit SH110X (+ GFX, BusIO), RTClib, Adafruit GPS Library,
      Adafruit BNO08x, Adafruit MAX1704X, Adafruit BMP3XX, Adafruit SHT31,
      Adafruit PCF8574, Adafruit ADS7830 (+ Unified Sensor). SD, WiFi,
      WebServer, ESP-NOW and ETH come with the core; the 24LC32, the LCD's
      MCP23008, the AW9523, the DRV2605L and the INA260 are driven directly
      over Wire.
    For an update over Wi-Fi: upload the release's VMR_vX.Y.Z.vmr, or a build
      of your own: Sketch > Export Compiled Binary, then VMR.ino.bin from the
      build folder (not .merged, .bootloader or .partitions). A release's
      .vmr, .wtb and .iwb are the boards' app images (.ino.bin), named for
      their board.

  PHYSICAL CONTROLS (a one-page guide: VMR_Controls.docx)
    Knob (pot) ......... brightness of every display and LED (or the
                         dashboard's slider: "Brightness from the knob" in
                         the Displays card)
    Knob switch ........ all displays on / off (acts when flipped; the dashboard's
                         On / Off works in between)
    Screen button ...... let go: the next OLED screen ticked in the Displays card. With the
                         OLED off: the LCD's (or 7-segment's) next ticked screen
    Screen button held 1 s (OLED on): the OLED dims to 30 % and shows KNOB SEL
      n/11 and what letting go will do; the knob's position picks (no knob:
      the slider's level). 11 even bands, about 9 % each:
        1  restart the Wi-Fi
        2  Wi-Fi off / on (off until switched on or a restart)
        3  show the Wi-Fi name, password and link (any button: back)
        4  the tilt ball (and back)
        5  flip the OLED and the 7-segment 180 deg (and back)
        6  a countdown on the 7-segment from the last time set (5:00 at
           first; again: stop it)
        7  pause / resume (a tap of the screen button or B resumes too)
        8  start / stop recording (the last condition and file split)
        9  the OLED off (RAM): only the screen button brings it back (that
           press does nothing else), so the knob is free for the
           7-segment's brightness; B and Mark work as usual; the 7-segment,
           LCD and LEDs carry on. A screen tapped in the Displays card brings it back too
        10 GPS off / on (on = a hot start)
        11 cancel: nothing happens
      With SeaLabMOS ticked there are 12 bands (about 8 % each): Cast session
      (arm / disarm the WTB and IWB) comes 9th, after recording.
      Restarting or switching off the Wi-Fi, recording, the GPS off while
      recording and disarming with a cast open ask first: press the screen
      button again within 5 s; Mark, B or waiting cancels. Wi-Fi jobs run in
      their own task, so the loop never waits on them.
    Screen button held + tap Mark: the 7-segment steps to its next ticked
      reading (the OLED says which); no mark, and letting go does nothing.
    Mark button ........ press = a location mark; hold 1.5 s = start / stop a
                         cast (with a session armed, the CTD cast). In
                         Datalogger mode it wakes the VMR instead.
    OLED button B ...... next screen at once; resumes from Pause; wakes the VMR
                         from Datalogger and Charge mode.
    Optional buttons (the Pin mappings card): Cast (as Mark held), Record (asks
      first: press again within 5 s), Pause, Wi-Fi hotspot (asks before
      switching it off), Screen jump (straight to the screen picked in
      the Pin mappings card).
    Any of them (and a knob turn) while an alarm shows: acknowledges it and does
      nothing else; any of them also restarts the BNWAS period (see ALARMS).
    WTB (SeaLabMOS_WTB): six Niskin buttons, each held 1.5 s fires its armed
      bottle; the rings and the green capacitor-ready LED.

  BOOT
    The Adafruit logo, the Aspect Wave LLC logo (2 s each), then the boot
    list: "Vessel Measurement Recorder vX.XX", then each part as it starts
    (name, OK / FAIL), held 2 s (12 s if anything failed). All at 50 %
    brightness even with the displays switched off. About 12 s from power-on
    to the first screen, Health.

  OLED SCREENS
    The screen button steps through those ticked in the Displays card (kept in EEPROM;
    by default all but Maxima, Sky, Dock and Tilt ball). A screen with nothing
    to show is skipped and greyed out in the Displays card: the motion screens with the
    BNO085 off, Position / GPS / Sky with the GPS off, Wind without the wind
    meter, Weather / Wx graph without the add-ons. Temperatures and lengths
    are in the System card's units (default F and ft).
    1  Live: local time (ZD), roll / pitch / rate of turn with their 1-min max
    2  Graph: roll over the chosen length (limits from the Graph card); at 5 and
       10 min an amplitude envelope (highest / lowest within about one roll)
    3  Position: local and UTC time, lat / lon, averaged SOG / COG, satellites
       used / in view and best signal, HDOP, fix age, altitude
    4  Maxima: all-time maxima from EEPROM with the date each was set
    5  GPS: the fix line, then the position in large letters
    6  Clinometer: the needle is the live roll on the Graph card's scale (+/-10-60
       deg; under 40 stretched to zoom in), with drag pointers at the
       largest roll each way since reset
    7  Location marks: newest first - number (5C = cast 5), LT, lat / lon, a
       cast's duration and drift (or OPEN)
    8  Sky plot: every satellite in view by elevation and azimuth, north up;
       filled = used in the fix, bigger = stronger
    9  Dock: trim (by the stern / head) and list to 0.01 deg, means over the
       dock card's window; "UNCAL" until calibrated from the drafts
    10 G-meter: the felt push in the deck plane (up = forward, right =
       starboard; gravity included), with a trail, peak-hold ticks and
       vertical g on a bar (1 g = still)
    11 Waves: the roll, pitch and heave spectra against period (log, 2-35 s);
       header: the heave Hs (VH at the VMR, MH at midships), RP (roll peak)
       and PP (pitch peak, the encounter period); a dotted line and label at
       the stronger peak.
       The last estimate is kept (OLD m:ss) while the window refills; none at
       the dock, paused or with the IMU off
    12 Wave statistics: the pitch and roll spectrum peaks, heave Hs and its
       peak period, the pressure heave Hs, the felt roll period, and LIVE /
       OLD / time left
    13 Wi-Fi devices: the last 4 phones / laptops, on now (filled dot, time
       joined) or gone (hollow, time left), and the weakest signal on now
    14 Wind: true wind (1-s mean) large with a dial, the 2-min mean, the
       apparent wind and its 10-min gust; MC = motion corrected. Without a
       GPS fix, the apparent wind
    15 Weather: pressure (mbar, inHg) and its 3-h change (the last
       5 min's mean less the mean of the 5 min 3 h before), air temperature,
       humidity and dew point
    16 Weather graph: pressure, air temperature and humidity over the Weather
       card's span (5 min - 3 h), a gap where there was no reading
    17 Tilt ball: a ball rolls the way the VMR leans; keep it in the ring.
       Time in the ring and the best (EEPROM). Turn ball 90 deg in the Displays card
       if it rolls the wrong way for how the OLED sits
    18 Cast: only while a cast is open - the OLED switches to it when a cast
       starts and back when it stops. Number, start time, elapsed time
       (large), drift from the start (distance, true bearing), SOG / COG
    19 Recording: REC and time recording (or PAUSED / not recording), file,
       rows, size, log rate, gaps, rows dropped, rewrites, microSD state.
       Not recording: how to start (dashboard, or the knob's Recording
       position)
    20 Health (the boot screen): LT with its source (G GPS, R RTC, W a phone
       on the Wi-Fi), uptime; battery V, %, charging / USB; time left on the
       battery or to full while charging, and the draw in mA (see BATTERY);
       brightness, CPU and box temperature; phones on the Wi-Fi (or OFF) and
       the weakest signal; the Fault LED's reasons, one at a time
    21 CTD: only while a SeaLabMOS session is armed - the IWB's
       state, depth (large), temperature, salinity, density, the cast
       number and the supply at the IWB (NO WTB / IWB link down when lost)
    22 Roll: the roll alone, large, no PORT / STBD (as the
       7-segment). It and the Clinometer are redrawn 10 times a second, the
       others 5 (the tilt ball 12.5)
    23 Download: only while the dashboard's download pop-up is open
       (or the VMR sends a file): the file, how far, how fast
    24 Voyage: only with an active route (the Chart tab; see ROUTES) - the
       waypoint ahead, its rhumb-line bearing and distance, cross-track
       distance and side, time to go and ETA (LT) at the averaged SOG, and
       the next waypoint and course, as the Chart tab's Own ship card
    Over every screen when they apply: an alarm (ALARMS; even with the
    displays switched off), PAUSED, firmware update progress,
    IMU NOT FOUND, the serial monitor, MARK / CAST START / CAST STOP (2.5 s),
    the screen-button hold panel. A STEMMA power-cycle or Reset I2C bus sets
    the OLED up again on the screen it was on.

  7-SEGMENT AND LCD (the Displays card)
    7-segment: one reading - roll, pitch, ROT, SOG, COG, time LT, battery,
    mbar (to 0.1 with the thousands dropped: 013.2 = 1013.2), air temp,
    humidity, wind kn, roll period, GM, heave Hs ("H1.25"), or a countdown
    (MM:SS, set in the Displays card only; at zero a light show, then dark). Flipped,
    it shows no decimal points.
    LCD (20x4, all 4 rows): motion, position, SOG / COG, weather, wind,
    waves, time, battery / recording, maxima, Cast (cast number and
    duration, the CTD's depth / temperature / salinity or the drift, the six
    bottles and the capacitor, the next planned depth or fired / armed
    counts; shown with a cast open or a session armed), or cycling roll /
    position / weather every 5 s. The Cast screen comes up once by itself
    when a cast opens. Backlight colour from presets or the Add-ons card's mixer.
    Both have tick boxes for what the buttons step through; a reading whose
    sensor is off shows ----.

  BATTERY
    3.7 V LiPo (default): the MAX17048 gives the cell voltage and its own %
    and rate. Its % reads far low on these cells (20 % with 45 h left, 0 %
    with 4.7 h left on a measured run), so on the battery the % comes from
    the voltage instead: one-minute means, the median of the last 3, through
    a discharge curve measured on a 2500 mAh Adafruit LiPo at about 27 mA
    (0 % = 3.30 V, 20 % = 3.575 V). Time left = % over the slope of a
    straight line through up to the last 4 h of that % (from 20 min on;
    started again when the VMR changes its own load), and
    the draw in mA = that slope x the Ah in the System card; until then, the draw
    measured last time. On USB, and for 3 min after it comes out, the
    gauge's own % and rate (a charging cell sits near 4.2 V whatever it
    holds). USB in / out is seen from a step of 0.04 V in cell voltage: a
    step counts only if it clears all three readings before it and holds on
    the next, and none is taken for 30 s after the VMR changes its own load
    (Wi-Fi, CPU 240 / 80 MHz, Datalogger, displays, GPS). Below 20 % the
    Fault LED flashes; that clears only on a real charge (USB seen and the
    gauge's rate positive). At 3.30 V (a minute of it, on the battery; in
    Datalogger sleep, the reads made while awake for about 3 rows) the
    VMR shuts down cleanly: a SeaLabMOS session is disarmed, the recording
    gets a BATTERY EMPTY row and is stopped and closed, settings are saved,
    then everything off with no wake: it stays off until the power switch
    is cycled (or RESET). The CSV keeps the gauge's own % as batt_gauge_pct.
    12 V battery (SeaLabMOS, INA260): volts and amps measured; % counted
    from the amps against the System card's capacity (started from the chemistry's
    resting-voltage curve, reset to full when charging tapers; without a
    capacity, the curve alone). Chemistry: LiFePO4, Li-ion 3S, lead-acid.
    Low = under 20 % or at the chemistry's floor (LiFePO4 12.0 V, Li-ion 3S
    10.8 V, lead-acid 11.9 V), never while charging; it clears at 21 % and
    0.1 V above the floor. Low flashes the Fault LED and shows LOW in card
    18 (JSON bLow).

  WI-FI DASHBOARD
    Join "Vessel Measurement Recorder XXXX" (XXXX = the end of this unit's
    device ID; password AP_PASS) and browse to http://192.168.4.1. Each unit
    takes its own channel (1, 6 or 11). No internet: if the phone offers to
    use cellular instead, choose to stay connected. Up to 4 phones at once.
    With no phone for 1 min the CPU drops to 80 MHz; a phone joining brings
    240 MHz back within half a second.
    Top bar: the tab's name (phone) or VMR and the version, status chips
      (recording, GPS, battery, SeaLabMOS; a tap goes to the card), the local
      time and date, the theme (Day / Night / Red), Mark (its menu: start /
      stop cast, an event note in the recording) and the more menu: UTC,
      battery, uptime, Pause, At the dock, Datalogger, Charge mode and the ZD
      selector. ZD uses the nautical sign (UTC = LT + ZD). Until GPS time
      arrives, an open page sets the clock (and RTC) from the phone, and once
      per boot the ZD from its time zone.
    Tabs: on a wide screen grouped tabs across the top (Live, Log, SeaLabMOS,
      then Setup and Tools at the right) with sub-tabs under them; on a phone
      up to 4 tabs in a bottom bar and the rest under More. Each sub-tab has a
      left and a right column of cards (side by side on a wide screen, the
      left above the right on a phone). The Tabs card moves groups, tabs,
      sub-tabs and cards by dragging; the layout is kept per phone. A tab
      shows only while one of its cards applies.
    Each card's how-to text is behind its "Show instructions" button; only
    readings and status show. The first visit shows a welcome box (About in
    the footer shows it again), then asks the vessel class once.
    The cards, by tab (the default layout):
    Motion
      At the dock (the more menu; replaces the GPS & motion card at the top; every phone
        follows it; the wave analysis is off): trim = L x tan(pitch) and list,
        means over 30 s - 5 min; calibrate from the drafts read at the marks
      GPS & motion: position, SOG / COG averaged over the GPS settings and diagnosis card's setting
        (COG from the average velocity, -- when too slow); roll (PORT / STBD),
        rate of turn, pitch; the Mag row (magnetic variation, magnetic heading
        and deviation, see MAGNETIC); roll period, significant roll and
        largest roll over the Maxima, roll statistics and GM card's window
      Graph / Clino / G views of the live data: roll (left axis, starboard
        up) and pitch (right axis, bow up) over 30 s - 10 min with settable
        limits (shared with the OLED graph); the clinometer and G-meter as on
        the OLED, with their resets and scales
      Maxima, roll statistics and GM: last 1 min and all-time maxima; roll
        statistics over 10 or 30 min (median period, significant roll = mean
        of the highest third, largest, RMS, mean list, count, count beyond a
        set angle); GM (see GM)
    Weather
      Weather (with the add-ons on): pressure, sea-level pressure (needs keel
        to bridge and drafts), 3-h change, air temperature, humidity, dew
        point; a graph of one at a time over 5 min - 3 h; wind and its rose
        with the wind meter
      Waves: every 30 s from the last 17 min (first after 8.5 min): the
        encounter period (pitch spectrum peak), the roll period (roll spectrum
        peak), heave Hs from the motion and from the pressure (see PRESSURE
        HEAVE) and the vessel heave avg (their mean) - at midships when the
        VMR's position is set, with the VMR's beside it; the ship's heave,
        not the wave height. The spectra (2-35 s), Reset and Save image; the
        learned pressure gain by period, Forget learned gain, and the
        advanced settings (coherence, blend band, learning rate)
    Chart
      Chart: as on Marks (below), larger, with Pop out (a window of its own on
        a laptop) and the COG vector (0-120 min, a tick every 6 min); the
        anchor watch (see ALARMS)
      Own ship: position, COG, SOG; with an active route the voyage (to
        waypoint, bearing, distance, time and ETA at the SOG, cross-track
        distance, next waypoint and course)
      Route: the active route (the same for everyone; see ROUTES), new, edit
        (tap the chart for waypoints, drag them, name them), import and export
        GPX, delete, leg table (each waypoint's ETA at the SOG), course and
        distance labels on the legs, planned speeds
      Voyage calculation: distance along the route from the ship or a waypoint
        to a waypoint; time and ETA at the SOG and at the planned speeds;
        speed, time to go or ETA, one typed and the others from it
    Marks
      Location marks: MARK, Start / Stop cast, the table (time, note, lat /
        lon, COG / SOG, weather, duration, drift), Save to Phone, Copy, New
        file, Delete last, Clear; the open cast at the bottom
      Chart: where the VMR is, its track (1 h to the hours kept; Clear track), the marks
        and the active route;
        NOAA charts from the chart packs on the card (see CHARTS), and street,
        satellite, topo and OpenSeaMap tiles when the phone also has
        internet; with neither, a latitude / longitude grid (see TRACK)
    Recording
      Recording: START / STOP, Save Last 20min, this recording's set-up
        (loading condition, or the tow for a towboat; voyage number; file
        name; one file or one a day; Datalogger: GPS awake until a fix),
        logging rate, Mark event; the optional column groups (Columns)
      microSD files: Card / Archive / Plots / Marks tabs (and the WTB's
        card with SeaLabMOS); download (one file, or a .zip), delete, archive
        and back. Only while not recording
      Plot from file: a file and span, the items (roll, pitch, battery V, SOG,
        roll period, GM, trim, list, pressure, air temp, humidity, wind, wind
        dir, 5-min mean); the VMR reduces the file to 600 columns in an
        idle-priority task and the phone draws the PNG. Other plots: GM plot
        against a CargoMax GM, Waves, Weather, Wind rose. Save to System
        (/plots) or Phone
    SeaLabMOS (while it is on)
      Cast session: Arm / Disarm, Tare, Start / Stop CTD cast, how casts
        start and stop, switch off after 20 min with no cast; under it the
        Location marks and Chart cards, the CTD profile beside them
      CTD profile (SeaLabMOS): the live trace of the open cast, the
        last cast's profile (temperature, salinity, density by depth), its
        file, the IWB's and WTB's state and events
      Fire control (Niskin bottles): six bottles - arm, fire (asks
        first), safe, reset, planned depths and the lowering speed; the
        capacitor's charge and recharge time; what each fired bottle closed
        at. A request needs the session armed and the WTB answering (
        the VMR refuses it otherwise, 409)
    Setup
      Level zero and ship data (with Ship saves: export / import / delete,
        in /.ship): vessel class (Alaska-class fills in its
        dimensions); this voyage (drafts fwd / mid / aft, displacement LT /
        MT, TPI, Cb, Cwp, parallel WL, the loading computer's GM); the barge
        (towboat); level zero (Set / Clear / 0.1 deg arrows; with drafts it
        sets the deck slope they give); the ship (beam, LOA, LBP, keel to
        deck / bridge / top); where the VMR is (from midships, the centreline,
        above the keel). Shows the implied Cb / Cwp, draft, hog / sag,
        freeboard, air draft and the GM working
      Displays: On / Off and brightness for all, then each display fitted:
        OLED screens (tick = in the rotation; tap = show now), Flip, tilt
        ball settings; 7-segment readings, Flip, countdown; LCD screens,
        backlight and a copy of its 4 rows
      Add-ons: the BNO085 on / off and its mounting (chip faces, X arrow,
        flips); each add-on with its address and status (BMP388, SHT, PCF8574,
        ADS7830, AW9523, the vibration motor); the SHT30's heater (on / off,
        Heat now); the displays; the wind meter's rate, north mark, the north
        mark's heading for under 1 kn, height and offset; SeaLabMOS (pair / unpair the WTB, Niskin
        bottles, the laptop port); Scan
      Pin mappings: each control and LED (Feather, PCF8574 or AW9523 pins),
        the LCD backlight pins, Test LEDs, the GPIO and ADC channels live;
        the optional buttons (Cast, Record, Pause, Wi-Fi hotspot,
        Screen jump and its screen), each None until picked; the piezo's
        one or two pins
      Charts: the NOAA chart packs on the card (area, zooms, NOAA's date,
        size), Load a pack (.vmrchart, only while not recording), Delete,
        where to get packs (see CHARTS); Track settings (see TRACK)
      GPS settings and diagnosis: lat / lon format (this phone), module
        status, fix, instantaneous SOG / COG / altitude, satellites, signal,
        antenna, HDOP; Restart, Cold start, Turn off / on; sky plot; the
        SOG / COG average (1-60 s, default 10)
      System: restart and version, battery type (3.7 V LiPo or 12 V INA260)
        with its chemistry and capacity (Ah), units (F / C, feet / metres,
        kn / m/s / km/h, nm / km), Wi-Fi health, the last 4 devices (name
        this phone), memory and web timing; Show hidden files; Settings
        backups (back up, restore, delete, in /.cfg); Restart VMR, Restart
        Wi-Fi, Reset I2C bus, Reset maxima, Clear EEPROM (every setting and
        the maxima, then a restart into the defaults)
      Firmware update (password): upload VMR_vX.Y.Z.vmr or VMR.ino.bin (or, with SeaLabMOS,
        WTB_vX.Y.Z.wtb or IWB_vX.Y.Z.iwb, or their .ino.bin, relayed); the VMR checks it, writes the spare
        slot, verifies and restarts (about 15 s); recording stops, settings
        stay (Clear EEPROM erases them once the new firmware is in). The OLED
        shows the progress and old --> new version
      Alarms: each alarm's output (Off, Visual, Sound, Vibrate, both) and
        limit, the BNWAS period, Save, Test, Silence (see ALARMS)
      Tabs: drag groups, tabs, sub-tabs and cards; phone bar, start tab,
        sub-tabs in the top row; Default layout
    Tools
      Hardware check: crash / watchdog / brownout restarts (with the loop's
        position and a backtrace), I2C parts not answering, IMU recoveries,
        I2C read failures by device, microSD rows written again, loop stalls
      Serial monitor: the last 10 lines (30 expanded); Show on OLED
      Diagnostics: internal RAM and PSRAM free (with an hour's graph), each
        task's unused stack, a microSD speed test, the last 16 GPS sentences

  MODES (the top bar's more menu)
    PAUSE: for moving the VMR. Live values update, but no rows are logged and
      nothing is counted (maxima, roll period, statistics hold); the waves
      and GM start again 8.5 min after Resume. PAUSED / RESUMED events in the
      file. Resume on the page or with B. Not kept across a restart.
    DOCK: the At the dock card at the top and OLED screen 9; wave analysis
      off; AT DOCK / UNDERWAY marked in the file. Kept in EEPROM.
    LOGGER (Datalogger, only while recording): Wi-Fi and the OLED off, CPU
      slowed, red LED blip every 5 s. At 1 min and slower (IMU off) it sleeps
      between rows (light sleep; the file closed and reopened around each),
      the GPS in standby, waking 20 s before each row for a fresh fix. With
      "keep the GPS awake until each row has a fix" ticked at Start (the Recording card,
      kept in EEPROM) it stays awake until the GPS has a fix, at most 2 min
      past the row. DATALOGGER ON / OFF events (with how the GPS is run) go in
      the file, and its header says whether it was on at the start. The screen button shows
      the countdown to the next row; B or Mark leaves it. Not kept across a
      restart.
    CHARGE (confirmed first): everything off and deep sleep, to charge as fast
      as possible; the Feather's charge LED goes out when full. B, the screen
      button on Feather pin 12, or off / on wakes it (a fresh boot).
    After a crash (panic or watchdog) a recording resumes in the same file
    with a CRASH RESTART event; after a power loss it does not. An armed
    SeaLabMOS session is kept through a reset (RTC memory), not a power-up.

  IMU (BNO085 on I2C)
    Roll and pitch from the Game Rotation Vector (gyro + accelerometer, no
    magnetometer), at 50 Hz. The BNO085 stretches the I2C clock and can drop
    out: every report is read through imuEventHandler(); a silent IMU is
    asked again for its reports, then the STEMMA QT rail is power-cycled
    (lines held low, so it really resets), with longer waits each time; a
    write it cannot take is abandoned after 0.25 s. Counted in the Hardware check card. If
    it does not start at boot, IMU NOT FOUND shows until it does.
    Mounting (the Add-ons card): which way the chip faces (up, down, bow, stern, port,
    starboard) and where the X arrow points; reports are turned into ship
    axes as they arrive. Changing it clears level zero and the trim
    calibration.
    Ship axes: X bow, Y port, Z up. Roll S = starboard down (CSV +), P = port
    down (-); pitch + = bow up; rate of turn + = turning to starboard. Check
    once and tick a flip in the Add-ons card for any that is backwards.

  MAGNETIC
    Variation: NOAA's World Magnetic Model 2025 at the GPS position (valid to
    the end of 2029), or the GPS's own RMC field if it gives one; kept in
    EEPROM so the last stands until a fix. Magnetic heading: the BNO085's
    calibrated field, tilt corrected, with the ship's deviation in it.
    Deviation = COG - magnetic heading - variation over the last minute at
    3 kn or more on a steady course (COG stands in for a gyro).

  GM
    GM = (2 C B / T)^2, C = 0.373 + 0.023 B/d - 0.043 L/100 (IS Code 2008,
    A 2.3), B beam, d quarter-mean draft, L LBP (LOA if none), T the peak of
    the roll spectrum (natural roll; the felt roll period mixes in forced
    rolling and reads GM high). None for the first 8.5 min of steady motion,
    at the dock or paused. A cross-check against the loading computer, not a
    stability figure. The CSV's gm_ft is the same value.

  PRESSURE HEAVE
    The BMP388 runs by itself (normal mode, 25 Hz, pressure x8, IIR 3) and is
    read every 100 ms. Its 0.5-s means become height, z = -(p - p0) / (rho g)
    (1 m is about 0.12 mbar; rho from the pressure and the air temperature,
    else the box's), with each 128-s segment's straight-line trend taken off,
    and go through the Waves analysis beside the acceleration: same 17-min
    window, same 1.6-20 s band, moved to midships the same way. Hs = 4 x the
    standard deviation. No double integration, so long swell holds up; wind
    gusts, doors and ventilation are in it. Needs the BMP388 for at least the
    newest 8.5 min of the window. The weather value is the mean of each second's readings.

  UNITS
    The System card: temperatures in F or C, lengths in feet or metres, speeds
    in kn, m/s or km/h and distances in nm or km, on every display and the
    dashboard (default F, feet, kn and nm). The CSV files keep their own
    units, named in each column. Pressure in mbar; the CTD in SI (dbar, m,
    degC, PSS-78, kg/m3).

  SD LOG
    Written only between Start and Stop. Each Start opens
    /YYYYMMDD_HHMMSSZ[_name].csv (UTC at Start; /NOCLK_001_name.csv before
    the clock is set); one file per recording or a new one each day at
    00:00 UTC. Not resumed after a power loss.
    While not recording, the last 20 min go to 1-min files in /.buf; Save
    Last 20min joins them into <start>_last20min.csv.
    TRACK: with a GPS fix and the clock set, a point (UTC, lat, lon) every
    30 s while moving 15 m or more, else every 5 min (the Charts card's Track
    settings change these and the span kept: 24 h to 30 days, default 72 h,
    or All), appended to /.trk/track.bin (12 bytes each), the track's only
    home: nothing of it in RAM, so a restart keeps it. A span is cut back once
    its oldest point is a quarter of the span past it; All never is. The Chart
    card is sent at most about 50,000 points (a longer span thinned, the
    points either side of each gap kept). Clear track (the Chart card)
    empties it. The hidden folders (/.buf, /.trk, /.wave, /.ship, /.cfg) show
    in the microSD files card only with Show hidden files (System card).
    CHARTS: NOAA chart packs (.vmrchart) in /charts, loaded from Setup ›
    Charts; the Chart card shows them, the most detailed pack where they
    overlap. Packs for the Strait of Juan de Fuca and Puget Sound, the
    Washington outer coast, Southeast Alaska and San Francisco Bay are
    built from NOAA's Chart Display Service when NOAA updates it (the releases
    named charts-YYYY-MM-DD at github.com/sscopazzi/
    Vessel_Measurement_Recorder); charts/make_vmrchart.py
    makes one for any area from NOAA's MBTiles. Not for navigation.
    Rows wait in a 256 KB PSRAM buffer while the card is busy or failing; a
    failed card is remounted (2 s, then doubling to 10 s) and the backlog
    written. Rows the card dropped are written again from RAM (SD REWROTE);
    rows are lost only if the buffer fills (SD RECOVERED ... rows lost).
    Rate: 10 Hz on a fixed 100-ms grid with the BNO085 on; with it off also
    1 s - 10 min, rows on the clock, plus a row for each event.
    The file starts with # metadata lines (system, firmware, device, file,
    vessel and voyage set-up, start time and its source, ZD, rates, axes,
    level zero, ship data, dock calibration, notes on the derived columns).
    Read with pandas: pd.read_csv(path, comment='#'), or vmr_reader.py.
    Columns: utc_time, millis, roll_deg, pitch_deg, yaw_rate_dpm, gps_lat,
      gps_lon, gps_alt_m, gps_speed_ms, gps_speed_kn, gps_angle_deg, gps_fix,
      gps_sats, gps_hdop, gps_fix_quality, gps_age_ms, batt_v, batt_pct,
      zero_applied, roll_period_s, gm_ft, event, event_note, acc_fwd_g,
      acc_stbd_g, acc_up_g, pressure_mbar, air_temp_c, rh_pct, trim_ft,
      list_deg, wave_te_s, wave_tr_s,
      heave_hs_m, heave_tp_s, roll_tp_s, heave_hs_mid_m, mag_heading_deg,
      mag_field_ut, mag_cal, wind_app_deg, wind_app_kn, wind_true_deg,
      wind_true_kn, mag_var_deg, box_temp_c, baro_hs_m, baro_hs_mid_m,
      baro_tp_s, fused_hs_m, fused_hs_mid_m, fused_coh, baro_gain, iwb_sal_psu, iwb_temp_c, iwb_pres_dbar, iwb_cpu_c,
      iwb_depth_m, iwb_ec_uscm, iwb_rho_kgm3, iwb_int_t_c, iwb_int_rh_pct,
      iwb_dewpt_c, iwb_v, iwb_state, iwb_cast, batt_a, batt_gauge_pct
    Column groups can be left out (the Recording card's Columns, fixed for a
    recording at its Start): acceleration (acc_*), weather (pressure_mbar,
    air_temp_c, rh_pct, box_temp_c), trim (trim_ft, list_deg), waves (wave_*,
    heave_*, roll_tp_s, baro_*, fused_*), magnetic (mag_*), wind (wind_*),
    SeaLabMOS (iwb_*); the header's columns_left_out line names them.
    Blank means no value (no IMU data, sensor off, not yet measured). GPS
    columns are filled only on the first row after a new fix (never
    repeated); a lost fix is logged with gps_fix = 0. Slow columns (battery,
    level zero, roll period, GM, add-on weather, mag_cal, box_temp_c,
    batt_a) are written once a second and on change, blank in between
    (= unchanged). Wave and baro_ columns come on the row after each new
    estimate (every 30 s); mag_var_deg on the first row and when it changes.
    iwb_*: the WTB's 1-s means, on the row after the second they
    cover, never repeated; iwb_state on every row while SeaLabMOS is on;
    iwb_cast while a CTD cast is open. The 5 Hz CTD samples are on the
    WTB's card; each finished cast's profile also goes to /casts/ here.
    acc_*: specific force in ship axes, g (level and still = 0, 0, +1).
    Events: Mark event, LOCATION MARK n, CAST n START / STOP, PAUSED /
    RESUMED, DATALOGGER ON / OFF, set-up changes, TRIM CALIBRATED, LEVEL ZERO SET, WAVES RESET,
    SD RECOVERED / REWROTE, CRASH RESTART, the SeaLabMOS and Niskin events.
    Notes are ASCII, up to 40 characters.

  MICROSD FILE MANAGER (the microSD files card)
    Downloads and deletes only while not recording (enforced by the
    firmware). Several files download as one uncompressed .zip (up to 4 GB
    and ZIP_MAX_FILES files). Archive moves files to /archive (_2, _3 ... on
    a clash). Plot from file reads the top folder only. The live numbers
    pause during a download; one day at 10 Hz is roughly 80 MB, so for weeks
    of data pull the card.

  GPS
    1 Hz at 9600 baud with SBAS (GPS_RATE_HZ 5 or 10 switch to 115200 baud;
    10 Hz turns SBAS off and is slightly less accurate per fix). Speed is
    stored in m/s and shown in knots; COG is course over ground, not
    heading, and means nothing when stopped. The clock is set from GPS
    (median of 5 RMCs every 60 s, corrected when off by more than 0.6 s,
    eased into a recording's times) and kept in the RTC.

  ALARMS (the Alarms card)
    Each alarm's output: Off, Visual (the OLED shows ALARM over any screen,
    even with the displays off, the Fault LED blinks fast, the dashboard
    shows a bar across the top), or Visual and Sound (the piezo, 2.65 kHz),
    Vibrate (the DRV2605L; with none answering it sounds instead) or both.
    All off by default; none in Datalogger mode.
      Anchor watch (the Chart card): Drop anchor takes the GPS position now;
        the position is locked until the small lock button opens it for
        typing. Alarm outside the radius (5-5000 m, default 50; clears at
        95 % of it) or with no fix for 60 s. The distance is from the GPS
        antenna, not the bow: for small craft.
      Roll (default 25 deg) and true wind (35 kn): past the limit, held 10 s
        after the last time. Heave Hs (3 m): the estimate the screens show
        (fused, else the motion's, else the pressure's), clears at 95 %.
        Battery (20 %): below it and not charging, clears 2 % above. GPS
        lost (60 s): no fix that long with the GPS on.
      BNWAS: with no VMR control touched for the period (3-12 min, default
        12), 15 s of visual alarm, then sound and vibration that get faster
        (2 s, then 1 s after 30 s, then 0.4 s two-tone after 60 s). Only a
        control on the VMR resets it, never the dashboard. Off at the dock.
    A VMR control acknowledges every alarm showing and the press does
    nothing else; the dashboard's Silence acknowledges all but BNWAS. An
    acknowledged alarm still on sounds again after 5 min. Sound patterns:
    the anchor and GPS high-low, the others three short beeps every 1.5 s.

  PROGRAM STRUCTURE
    loop() (the Arduino task, priority 1 like the web task: at 2, phones
      drop off the hotspot) reads
      the GPS and IMU, keeps time, draws the displays and makes the log rows.
      It is the only task on the I2C bus, and owns the relay and the
      SeaLabMOS link's state (slm.ino).
    sdTask writes the recording's rows to the card: loop() puts
      each row in the ring buffer (pend) and wakes it, so a slow card holds
      up only sdTask, never the 10-Hz rows. Opening, the 60-s checkpoint,
      remounting and the SD events are all done there. Start, Stop and the
      daily file still drain the buffer from loop() (once each).
    webTask serves the dashboard. Changes from the page go into req*
      (and slmReq*) variables and are applied by loop() in
      applyWebRequests(), so only one task touches I2C, the displays or the
      settings image P. webTask also owns the location marks (RAM list and
      file); loop() only signals the Mark button (reqMarkBtn) and draws what
      webTask prepares (markOled, castOled). Firmware updates are written to
      flash by webTask. The ESP-NOW receive callback only queues frames.
    plotTask (Plot from file) is made at boot with its stack in PSRAM and
      sleeps until a plot is asked for; it runs at loop()'s priority, taking
      turns. All SD access goes through sdLock(). Wi-Fi off / on / restart run in their own task
      (each calls battLoadChange(), as do the CPU-speed, Datalogger,
      display and GPS changes).
    Log replaces Serial: everything printed also goes to a 30-line copy for
      the serial monitor card and the OLED.
    Settings and all-time maxima live in P (struct Persist), saved to the
      EEPROM by servicePersist(); layouts V12-V33 are migrated on load to
      the current one (34), V3-V11 start from the defaults.
    Static RAM (dram0) is nearly full: large buffers go on the heap, in PSRAM
      when there is some (ps_malloc / heap_caps_malloc), not static.
    crumb(ST_...) records what loop() is doing in RTC memory, so after a
      watchdog restart the Hardware check card can say where it was stuck.
    The dashboard page and logo are kept readable in web/index.html and
      web/logo.svg; web/make_page.py gzips them into the byte arrays at the
      end of this file. Edit them, run it, compile.
    Arduino's prototypes: a function defined above the structs it uses moves
      every generated prototype above them and the build fails; use a macro
      there (awPin), or keep the structs near the top. slm.ino's globals
      are not visible in this tab: use its accessors (slmBtGet).
    "---- ZIPCORE BEGIN / END ----" marks code that off-device tests extract;
      keep them.
  ============================================================================
*/

